  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nPatchy frost, and perhaps a freeze, possible this weekend:\n\nAs the cold front dives through the Gulf, a rather abnormal high\npressure system (~99.5th percentile for this time of year) will\nmove through our region over the course of the weekend. This\nwill keep highs cooler (well actually quite seasonable) in the\n60s Saturday and Sunday. With calmer winds and clear skies\nbeneath the high, though, lows will be colder, dropping into the\n30s. This will bring a frost potential both Saturday and Sunday\nmornings, but especially Sunday morning when winds are forecast\nto be calmest, and where the eastern Ozarks are forecast to \nrange from 30-34 F.\n\nCurrent Frost probabilities (using <35 F as a proxy) are 15-40%\nalong and north of I-44, and in the eastern Ozarks, on Saturday\nmorning. Then, a 50-95% chance east of Highway 5 on Sunday\nmorning.\n\nCurrent Freeze probabilities (<32 F) are only pockets of 5-10%\nSaturday morning (mainly in valleys). Then, a 20-50% chance in\nthe eastern Ozarks Sunday morning.\n\nFor some context, the average first frost date for the eastern\nOzarks ranges from Oct. 10-15, while the average first freeze\ndata ranges from Oct. 17-22. So while we have had quite warm\ntemperatures September and the first half of October, the first\nfrost/freeze is actually coming around right on time in terms of\nclimatology. \n\n\nTemperatures quickly rebound to above normal into next week:\n\nThose fall-like temperatures will not last long, though, as\nglobal ensembles are in pretty good agreement of a ridge\namplifying over the region in response to a deep low digging \ninto the western CONUS. Temperatures are forecast to warm into\nthe 70s on Monday, reaching around 80 F by Wednesday. CPC agrees\nwith the current forecast, giving us a 70-80% chance of above\nnormal temperatures from October 20-24.",
